    {
      "id": "6QjPaDSJYRqdnDCQH3xBX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1853.193589438556,
      "y": 907.4920315941843,
      "width": 524.1239013671876,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 442597561,
      "version": 1490,
      "versionNonce": 214532893,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1776804090144,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Computers' ability to determine syntactic equivalence\n(2 == 2, \"Monday\" == \"Monday\") is basically perfect\n\nBefore LLMs, we had very few ways to determine\nsemantic equivalence (\"three days after Friday\" ==\n\"the first weekday\")\n\nLLMs can do this, but it is far from perfect, and\nunreliable.\n\nAn alternative to calculating semantic equivalence\ndirectly with an LLM is to first convert the natural\nlanguage to computer language, and then compare the\ncomputer language\n\nIs it more effective to send two sets of natural\nlanguage to an LLM to determine semantic\nequivalence, or is it better to first convert it to\ncomputer language (if possible) and then determine\nsyntactic equivalence? Which is more useful? Are\nthere certain scenarios where the second option is\nnot possible?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Computers' ability to determine syntactic equivalence (2 == 2, \"Monday\" == \"Monday\") is basically perfect\n\nBefore LLMs, we had very few ways to determine semantic equivalence (\"three days after Friday\" == \"the first weekday\")\n\nLLMs can do this, but it is far from perfect, and unreliable.\n\nAn alternative to calculating semantic equivalence directly with an LLM is to first convert the natural language to computer language, and then compare the computer language\n\nIs it more effective to send two sets of natural language to an LLM to determine semantic equivalence, or is it better to first convert it to computer language (if possible) and then determine syntactic equivalence? Which is more useful? Are there certain scenarios where the second option is not possible?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
  "appState": {
    "gridSize": 20,
    "gridStep": 5,
    "gridModeEnabled": false,
    "viewBackgroundColor": "#ffffff"
